<h2>Three routes to recovery</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>1. Direct payment — Section 20(6)</strong><p>The Magistrate directs the respondent&rsquo;s employer or debtor to pay the aggrieved person directly. Salary is intercepted at source; receivables are redirected before they reach the respondent. This is the fastest route where the respondent is salaried.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>2. Maintenance-style execution</strong><p>Through Section 28 DV Act and Rule 6(5) of the 2006 Rules, monetary orders are enforced the way maintenance orders are: a warrant to levy the amount as if it were a fine, attachment of movables, and imprisonment on persistent default — which punishes, but does not discharge, the arrears.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>3. Section 31 — for protective breaches</strong><p>Breach of a protection order or interim protection order is a cognizable, punishable offence. Its primary field is protective and residence directions; using it for bare money defaults is contested terrain, so it supplements rather than replaces the first two routes.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Section 20(6) in practice</h2>
<p>The direct-payment power is the DV Act&rsquo;s most underused tool. Its utility lies in bypassing the respondent altogether: once the employer is directed to remit a portion of salary to the aggrieved person, compliance no longer depends on the respondent&rsquo;s cooperation. Applications should identify the employer accurately — name, address, the respondent&rsquo;s designation and, where known, salary particulars — and ask that the direction continue month to month until arrears and current relief are satisfied. For self-employed respondents, the same clause reaches debtors: parties who owe the respondent money under contracts, rent or invoices can be directed to pay the aggrieved person instead.</p>

<h2>The execution route, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Compute arrears.</strong> A month-wise schedule from the date the order took effect, crediting any part-payments. Interim orders are enforceable from their own date unless the order says otherwise.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Apply to the trial Magistrate.</strong> The court that passed the order enforces it. Seek a recovery warrant and, in the alternative, Section 20(6) directions.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Warrant and attachment.</strong> The amount is levied as a fine would be — through attachment and sale of movables, with the distress machinery of the criminal code available.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Default imprisonment.</strong> On continued non-payment, the respondent may be sentenced to imprisonment for the default. Custody is coercive, not compensatory: release does not extinguish what is owed.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>One limitation period deserves attention: the maintenance machinery carries a first-proviso discipline requiring warrants to be sought within one year of the amount falling due. Enforcement applications under the DV Act are best filed promptly and renewed periodically rather than allowed to accumulate years of arrears before the first recovery step.</p>
</div>

<h2>Strategic choices</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>Salaried respondent:</strong> lead with Section 20(6). An employer direction is self-executing and embarrassingly visible, which itself induces compliance.</p>
<p><strong>Business respondent:</strong> combine the recovery warrant with disclosure — seek directions for the respondent to file an affidavit of income, assets and receivables, so that debtors and attachable assets are identified on oath.</p>
<p><strong>Parallel proceedings:</strong> where maintenance also runs under Section 144 BNSS or Section 24 HMA, keep the computation transparent across fora. Courts adjust overlapping amounts; enforcement credibility depends on clean accounting.</p>
</div>

